text:
Alice: Fyi I'm proposing a Terraform rollout to reduce GPU spend by increasing instance-type diversity + better interruption handling for spot pools. plan attached below.

Ben: :eyes: can you call out the canary steps? want to avoid a mass fallback to on-demand.

Alice: Canary plan: deploy to prod-us-west-2 only, new ASG with mixed_instances_policy + capacity_rebalance enabled; 10% of GPU capacity for 48h. If latency or OOM regressions >5% we rollback.

Carla: Sounds good. do we use allocation_strategy = "capacity-optimized" or "lowest-price"?

Alice: capacity-optimized for p3/p4/g5 mix. lowest-price caused too many interruptions in last test.

David: Also add a lifecycle hook + interruption handler so we drain pods and persist KV cache before instance termination. We should use instance metadata for the 2-minute spot notice on AWS.

Erin: I put together a tiny handler that calls our /drain endpoint and uploads logs to s3 then exits. snippet:```bash
# on-interrupt.sh
TOKEN=$(curl -s http://169.254.169.254/latest/meta-data/spot/instance-action || true)
if [ -n "$TOKEN" ]; then
  curl -X POST https://internal-api/agent/drain
  aws s3 cp /var/log/worker.log s3://redwood-spot-logs/$INSTANCE_ID.log
fi
```

Frank: We should also annotate instances for cost-center and owner so chargeback still works. tags: CostCenter=infra, Team=Serving, Env=prod, Role=gpu-worker.

Alice: Agreed. Terraform snippet for the launch_template + mixed policy (testing in canary):```hcl
resource "aws_launch_template" "gpu_lt_canary" {
  name_prefix = "gpu-canary-"
  image_id    = "ami-0abcd1234" # gpu AMI with nv driver
  instance_type = "g4dn.xlarge"
  tag_specifications = [{
    resource_type = "instance",
    tags = {
      CostCenter = "infra"
      Team       = "Serving"
      Env        = "canary"
    }
  }]
}

resource "aws_autoscaling_group" "gpu_asg_canary" {
  desired_capacity = 4
  mixed_instances_policy {
    launch_template {
      launch_template_specification { launch_template_id = aws_launch_template.gpu_lt_canary.id }
    }
    instances_distribution { on_demand_base_capacity = 0 }
    override { instance_type = "g4dn.xlarge" }
    override { instance_type = "p3.2xlarge" }
    override { instance_type = "g5.xlarge" }
  }
  capacity_rebalance = true
  lifecycle { create_before_destroy = true }
}
```

Ben: Nice. Make sure the AMI has the interruption handler systemd unit. Otherwise lifecycle hook won't get time.

Erin: Yep, baking AMI via Packer with a systemd service that runs on the metadata watch. Will push AMI id to the LT variable.

Carla: What about preemptible VMs in GCP regions? We've got some customers in europe-west1 that push workload.

Alice: For GCP: use preemptible = true on instance template and add a node-preemptible-handler sidecar to drain k8s pods. We'll keep europe-west1 as on-demand for now for higher-criticality customers.

David: Monitoring/alerts: create CloudWatch alarms on interrupted instance count + pending-scalar fallback events. If >3 interruptions in 5m for a zone, we disable that AZ in the ASG overrides.

Frank: Can we automate AZ removal? maybe a small controller that patches ASG overrides list.

Alice: We're adding a lambda that listens to CloudWatch Events for Spot Instance Interruption Notification and increments a per-AZ counter in DynamoDB; when threshold tripped it writes a terraform/ops-runbook ticket and triggers a canary rollback. Manual approval for removal.

SRE-Bot: Reminder: add SLO/latency heatmap to the rollout dashboard. link: https://console.redwood/boards/spot-rollout

deploy-bot: Terraform plan succeeded for gpu_asg_canary in prod-us-west-2 (apply pending approval).

David: @Alice please include rollback steps in the PR: set desired_capacity back to reserved baseline, remove capacity_rebalance if it causes thrash, and revert LT image to previous AMI id.

Alice: Will include in PR. tagging also includes "rollout_owner" = "alice" so support knows who to page.

Ben: Thx. once PR is up @David/Erin/Carla please review. target deploy tomorrow 0300 UTC canary window. :thumbsup:
